## Hello, and welcome!

I'm John Nkrumah Mensah, and I'm so glad you're here!\
I am interested in turning messy, real-world biological and environmental data into models that make predictions people can act on. My work sits at the intersection of data science, biology, and climate science, where I use quantitative tools to understand complex ecological and environmental problems.
Over the past years, my research has centered on using and developing spatio-temporal, quantitative, and mechanistic modeling approaches to evaluate the impacts of biotic and abiotic conditions on species populations and distribution.

What excites me most is turning complex data into insights that help us better understand the world around us. My research has taken me from long-term field studies and experiments to statistical modeling, machine learning, and spatial and temporal analysis. Along the way, I've built a broad toolkit spanning classical statistics (Bayesian methods, generalized additive models, mixed-effects models), functional data analysis, mathematical modeling, and machine learning.

This website is a window into my journey as a scientist, data analyst, and lifelong learner. Here, you'll find examples of my research, publications, projects, data science work, and the tools and ideas that continue to shape my thinking.

Whether you're an employer looking for a quantitative scientist or data scientist, a researcher interested in collaboration, or simply someone curious about my work, I invite you to explore. I hope you'll find something that sparks your interest – and perhaps an opportunity for us to work together, exchange ideas, or build something meaningful.
 

